Defer glAccum LOAD/ACCUM blending until GL_RETURN.

gl-repl's accumulation loop now starts with GL_LOAD, followed by up to
15 GL_ACCUM operations under one stable scissor.  Snapshot each completed
read framebuffer and cache its weight, then evaluate the weighted sum in
one fullscreen shader at GL_RETURN.  This removes the per-sample RGBA16F
read/modify/write chain while retaining the existing framebuffer snapshots
and their implicit WebGL canvas MSAA resolves.

The reduction shader is sized from the live fragment-sampler limit (rather
than gl4es's conservative Emscripten notest cache), capped at the app's
16 accumulation samples.  Sample textures are allocated lazily.  A changed
scissor, sampler-capacity overflow, GL_ACCUM without a leading GL_LOAD, or
an accumulation clear materializes the deferred batch through the original
RGBA16F/RGBA8 FBO path before continuing, preserving the generic emulation.

Performance measured 2026-08-24 in the in-app Chromium/WebGL2 browser at
1280x676, using the default gl-repl logo, 16x accumulation AA, and the
browser-selected 4x canvas MSAA: two interleaved 300-frame windows after a
120-frame warm-up improved the original accum-FBO path from 33.962 to
37.035 FPS (+9.05%).  Mean callback interval fell from 29.444 to 27.002 ms,
saving 2.443 ms per frame.  See packaging/web/patches/README.md for the
three-stage A/B and measurement caveats.

Applied by scripts/web-deps.sh immediately after gl4es-accum-fbo.patch.

Reduce-shader compile/link failure deletes the GL objects and sticks
so a later LOAD does not retry every frame. Dispose also frees sample
textures and the reduce program.

